{"ai_attack_steps": ["The attacker crafts a transaction targeting the `receive` function of the `TokenMapped` proxy contract.", "The attacker provides forged `Signature` structs. Crucially, the `signatory` field in each `Signature` struct is set to the address that will be used to recover the signature (which is the attacker's address in this case).", "The `receive` function recovers the signer's address using `ecrecover` with the provided signature components (v, r, s).", "The function then checks if the recovered address matches the `signatory` field in the `Signature` struct. Since the attacker set these to be the same, this check passes.", "The contract fails to perform any further authorization check on the `signatory`.", "The attacker is able to transfer funds from the `TokenMapped` contract by exploiting this flawed signature verification.", "The attacker uses 4 signatures in the exploit, likely to meet the `_minSignatures_` requirement set in the `MappingTokenFactory` contract, which is the factory for the `TokenMapped` contract."], "ai_attack_steps_text": "The attacker crafts a transaction targeting the `receive` function of the `TokenMapped` proxy contract. || The attacker provides forged `Signature` structs. Crucially, the `signatory` field in each `Signature` struct is set to the address that will be used to recover the signature (which is the attacker's address in this case). || The `receive` function recovers the signer's address using `ecrecover` with the provided signature components (v, r, s). || The function then checks if the recovered address matches the `signatory` field in the `Signature` struct. Since the attacker set these to be the same, this check passes. || The contract fails to perform any further authorization check on the `signatory`. || The attacker is able to transfer funds from the `TokenMapped` contract by exploiting this flawed signature verification. || The attacker uses 4 signatures in the exploit, likely to meet the `_minSignatures_` requirement set in the `MappingTokenFactory` contract, which is the factory for the `TokenMapped` contract.", "ai_confidence_has_source_code": true, "ai_confidence_known_pattern_match": true, "ai_confidence_reasoning": "2 verified, 1 proxy", "ai_confidence_score": 0.95, "ai_confidence_verified_contracts": true, "ai_explanation": "The attacker exploited a vulnerability in the `receive` function of the `TokenMapped` contract. The contract intended to verify signatures to authorize fund transfers. However, the verification logic was flawed: after recovering the signatory's address using `ecrecover`, it only checked if the recovered address matched the `signatory` field within the `Signature` struct (`require(signatory == signatures[i].signatory, "unauthorized");`). This check is redundant because `ecrecover` already returns the address that signed the hash. The crucial check to ensure the signatory was authorized (e.g., by checking against a list of authorized addresses or a role-based access control mechanism) was missing. Consequently, any valid signature could be used to bypass the authorization, allowing the attacker to drain funds.", "ai_mitigation": [], "ai_root_cause": "The `receive` function in `TokenMapped` incorrectly verifies signatures. It checks if the recovered signatory address matches the provided signatory address in the `Signature` struct, which is a redundant check. It fails to verify if the signatory is actually authorized to perform the action, allowing any valid signature to be accepted.", "ai_vulnerability_type": "Signature Replay", "attack_title": "Chainswap_exp1", "contracts": [{"abi_available": true, "address": "0x7fe68fc06e1a870dcbee0acae8720396dc12fc86", "artifact_dir": "data/contracts/2021_07_chainswap_exp1/contracts/01_vulnerable_7fe68fc0_initializableproductproxy", "bytecode_available": true, "chain": {"id": 1, "name": "Ethereum"}, "compiler_version": "v0.6.12+commit.27d51765", "contract_name": "InitializableProductProxy", "explorer