_confidence_known_pattern_match": true, "ai_confidence_reasoning": "1 verified, 0 proxy", "ai_confidence_score": 0.75, "ai_confidence_verified_contracts": true, "ai_explanation": "The vulnerability lies in the `listToken` function of the `XBridge` contract. This function allows users to register a token mapping between two different chain IDs. Crucially, the function does not properly validate the provided chain IDs. An attacker can exploit this by calling `listToken` with arbitrary chain IDs (e.g., 85936 and 95838) and a token address. The attacker also sends 0.15 ether as the listing fee. After successfully listing the token with these arbitrary chain IDs, the attacker can then call the `withdrawTokens` function. The `withdrawTokens` function, when withdrawing tokens, does not correctly verify the chain ID against the actual source chain. This allows the attacker to withdraw all tokens of the listed token from the `XBridge` contract to their own address, effectively bypassing the intended cross-chain logic and draining the contract's funds.", "ai_mitigation": [], "ai_root_cause": "The `listToken` function does not adequately validate the provided `baseToken.chain` and `correspondingToken.chain` IDs. This allows an attacker to register a token mapping with arbitrary, potentially non-existent or unverified, chain IDs. Subsequently, the `withdrawTokens` function fails to properly verify the chain ID context, enabling the attacker to withdraw tokens that were not legitimately bridged or processed through the intended cross-chain mechanisms.", "ai_vulnerability_type": "Improper Input Validation", "attack_title": "Tx Step2(withdrawToken): https://etherscan.io/tx/0x903d88a92cbc0165a7f662305ac1bff97430dbcccaa0fe71e101e18aa9109c92", "contracts": [{"abi_available": true, "address": "0x354cca2f55dde182d36fe34d673430e226a3cb8c", "artifact_dir": "data/contracts/2024_04_xbridge/contracts/01_vulnerable_354cca2f_xbridge", "bytecode_available": true, "chain": {"id": 1, "name": "Et